Tue Nov 17 16:43:40 UTC 2026 - Dirk Müller <dmueller@suse.com>

- update to 8.3:
  * NEW: `bre`: Add the binary properties `Any`,
    `Assigned`, and `ASCII`, which UTS #18 requires
    but the Unicode Character Database does not
    define. `ASCII` was previously only available as
    an alias of the `Basic_Latin` block.
  * NEW: `bre`: Allow an initial `Is` on General
    Category values (`p{IsLu}` or `p{gc=IsLu}`) and
    on property names (`p{IsGC=Lu}` or
    `p{IsAlphabetic=Yes}`), as Unicode allows, per
    UAX #44.
  * FIX: `bre`: Fix the inverse of the `No_Block`
    block property (`P{No_Block}`) not including the
    Supplementary Private Use Area blocks (`U+F0000`
    to `U+10FFFF`).
  * FIX: `bre`: Fix negated Unicode properties and
    POSIX classes (`P{Lu}`, `p{^Lu}`,
    `[[:^upper:]]`) matching both cases of the
    characters in the property when using
    `IGNORECASE`. Character sets that contain negated
    properties are now expressed as alternatives
    (`[aP{Lu}]` becomes `(?:[a]|[^...])`).
  * FIX: `bre`: Fix an error when negating a binary
    property alias that has a value (`P{Alpha=No}`).
- update to 8.2.1:
  * FIX: Reject compiled replace templates from
    another search pattern in compiled search
    objects, matching module-level substitutions and
    preventing incorrect capture references.
  * FIX: `bre`: Fix a `-` after a POSIX class in a
    character set sometimes forming a range
    (`[[:blank:]-z]`).
  * FIX: `bre`: Fix an escaped `-` before a POSIX or
    Unicode class in a character set being turned
    into a range operator (`[-p{N}]`).
  * FIX: `bre`: Fix `^` being treated as a negation
    when it follows properties in a character set
    that match nothing, such as `p{Han}` in a byte
    string (`[p{Han}^a]`).
  * FIX: `bre`: Fix ranges in byte string character
    sets with a named character beyond the byte range
    (`[a-N{CJK UNIFIED IDEOGRAPH-4E00}]`). They now
    match Regex: a range that ends beyond the byte
    range includes everything up to `xff`, and a
    range that starts beyond it matches nothing, or
    is an error if it is reversed.
  * FIX: `bre`: Fix a literal `^` after the negating
    `^` in a character set (`[^^]`) causing an
    unterminated character set error.
  * FIX: `bregex`: Fix finding the end of character
    sets, which could cause false errors or wrongly
    handle what follows a set (such as `R`, groups,
    or verbose comments). Character sets now follow
    Regex's own rules: POSIX classes are recognized
    in byte strings, and with qualified names or
    values (`[[:script=latin:]]`).
  * In V1, a `]` directly after a set operator is a
    literal (`[w--]]`).
  * A literal `^` after the negating `^` (`[^^]`) no
    longer causes an unterminated character set
    error.
  * FIX: `bregex`: POSIX classes in character sets
    are no longer rewritten before being passed to
    Regex.
- update to 8.2:
  * NEW: Drop Python 3.10.
  * FIX: Fix error in replace templates when casing
    a character escape gives two characters
    (`CxdfE`).
  * FIX: Fix wrong text or errors when case
    conversion of a `x` escape exceeds the byte
    range in replace templates.
  * FIX: Fix an empty format specification (`{1:}`)
    raising an error in format replace templates.
  * FIX: Fix Unicode properties, such as general
    category `p{L}` and `p{Bidi_Class=L}`, not
    matching ideographs, Hangul syllables, and other
    characters Unicode defines as ranges.
  * FIX: Fix `g<...>` references in replace templates
    silently dropping invalid characters in a group
    name instead of raising an error.
  * FIX: Fix `p{...}` Unicode properties rejecting
    the canonical dotted value form (e.g. `Age=1.1`),
    which previously only worked through the alias
    form (e.g. `V1_1`).
  * FIX: Fix issue where Unicode properties within
    character classes (e.g. `[x[:property:]]`) were
    incorrectly detected with `[x[l]]`
    (@rahul05ranjan).
  * FIX: Fix an undefined Unicode name in `N{...}`
    raising a `KeyError` instead of `PatternError`
- update to 8.1:
  * NEW: Add official support for Python 3.15.
- update to 8.0:
  * NEW: Backrefs will preemptively fail, raising a
    `PatternError`, if parsing and finding an
    unterminated character set (`[]`) or an
    unterminated subpattern (`()`). Both `re` and
    `regex` already do this (`re` just issues a
    warning on Python 3.10), but `backrefs` will now
    terminate parsing early instead of waiting for
    the underlying regular expression parsers to
    catch it after the fact. This will occur even on
    Python 3.10. If Python 3.10 behavior is
    required, please use Backrefs `7.0` until
    migration is possible.
  * NEW: Backrefs will now raise a `PatternError` in
    `bre` if global flag syntax is not used at the
    start of the pattern. Python `re` already does
    this, except on Python 3.10 where a warning is
    raised. Backrefs will now do this in `bre`, even
    on Python 3.10, to prevent backtracking in
    Backrefs' parser when global flags are redefined.
    If the old behavior is required, please use
    `7.0` until migration is possible.
  * NEW: Backrefs will now raise a `PatternError` in
    `bregex` if a global flag is not used at the
    start of the pattern. This does not affect
    inline flags that use the global syntax (e.g.
    `(?i)`) as `regex` allows this to be scoped to
    subpatterns (`(...)`) and affects the pattern
    after its insertion whether in a subpattern or
    outside a subpattern. While regex doesn't
    specifically restrict global flag insertion,
    only the last instance takes effect, Backrefs
    requires these global flags to be declared at
    the beginning of the pattern to prevent
    backtracking when parsing the pattern. If the
    old behavior is required, please use `7.0`
    until migration is possible.
  * NEW: Various `ValueError` and `SyntaxError` that
    used to be raised by the `bre` or `bregex`
    parser are now raised as
    `backrefs.util.PatternError`.
  * FIX: Fixes in `bregex` related to `regex`
    handling of inline flags using global syntax.

